WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.908
Not all animals have
a skeleton like yours.

2
00:00:07.128 --> 00:00:12.190
Some have a backbone, some have none,
and some wear a skeleton outside.

3
00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:16.591
You have a backbone. It is a
spine of small bones inside you.

4
00:00:16.811 --> 00:00:20.758
A dog has one too. So does a
bird, a fish, a frog and a snake.

5
00:00:20.978 --> 00:00:23.764
Animals with a backbone
are called vertebrates.

6
00:00:23.984 --> 00:00:29.279
Mammals, birds, fish, reptiles and
amphibians are all vertebrates. So are you.

7
00:00:29.779 --> 00:00:32.937
A worm has no backbone.
It has no bones at all.

8
00:00:33.157 --> 00:00:36.732
Neither does a slug, a
jellyfish or an octopus.

9
00:00:36.952 --> 00:00:40.250
Animals without a backbone
are called invertebrates.

10
00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.884
Most animals in the
world are invertebrates.

11
00:00:43.384 --> 00:00:46.821
Some invertebrates wear their
skeleton on the outside.

12
00:00:47.041 --> 00:00:52.103
It is a hard case over the whole
body, with joints so it can bend.

13
00:00:52.323 --> 00:00:56.363
It is called an exoskeleton.
Exo means outside.

14
00:00:56.583 --> 00:00:59.695
Insects, spiders, crabs
and woodlice have one.

15
00:00:59.915 --> 00:01:03.909
It protects them, and their
muscles pull on it from inside.

16
00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:09.841
A snail's shell is different. It is
not a backbone, and not an exoskeleton.

17
00:01:10.061 --> 00:01:15.587
The snail's soft body comes out of it, so
the shell does not cover the whole body.

18
00:01:16.087 --> 00:01:21.103
Scientists use a key to identify a
creature: to work out what it is.

19
00:01:21.323 --> 00:01:26.802
A key is a chain of yes-or-no questions.
Each answer leads to the next one.

20
00:01:27.022 --> 00:01:29.855
Look at this creature.
Does it have legs? Yes.

21
00:01:30.075 --> 00:01:34.023
Does it have exactly six legs?
Count them. Eight, so no.

22
00:01:34.243 --> 00:01:39.583
Does it have exactly eight legs?
Yes. The key names it: a spider.

23
00:01:39.803 --> 00:01:43.100
Answer from what you see, not
from what you think it is.

24
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:49.777
Today you sort ten animals by backbone.
Ask: is there a spine of bones inside it?

25
00:01:49.997 --> 00:01:56.777
A dog, a shark, a frog, a lizard,
a parrot: yes. Vertebrates.

26
00:01:56.997 --> 00:02:03.220
A snail, a spider, a jellyfish, a
bee, an earthworm: no. Invertebrates.

27
00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:07.527
Size is not the test. A giant
squid is an invertebrate.

28
00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:11.092
Then you look up how a
crab gets a bigger shell.

29
00:02:11.312 --> 00:02:16.931
The fact card is a secondary source: what
somebody else found out and wrote down.

30
00:02:17.151 --> 00:02:23.003
It says an exoskeleton cannot grow. So
the crab sheds it, and grows a bigger one.

31
00:02:23.223 --> 00:02:28.610
This is called moulting. The new case
is soft at first, so the crab hides.

32
00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:33.103
Vertebrates have a backbone
inside. Invertebrates do not.

33
00:02:33.323 --> 00:02:38.339
Some invertebrates wear an
exoskeleton. To grow, they shed it.

34
00:02:38.559 --> 00:02:42.599
A key names a creature, one
yes-or-no question at a time.

35
00:02:42.819 --> 00:02:46.581
Size and shells do not decide
it. Look for the backbone.
